Here in JVM :
-> there are two types of memory.
-> those are stack memory and heap memory.
-> stack follows lifo structure where the lastly stored data comes out first from the this memory.
-> whereas, heap follows open-space memory where all the data be stored at random space.

STACK :-
-> Every method along with main method in java code has its own stack memory.
-> now let us assume there is one method i.e, main() and another method in the class as add() method.
-> now the local variables or inside the one's method's local variables will be stored in their stack,
   But where will the instance variables in the class be residing?
     - "that's where the heap memory concept comes into the picture".

HEAP :-
-> whenever we create an obj(reference variables) with new keyboard
-> that obj's referenced variable will be stored into heap with having an unique address for that.
-> and the stored obj in heap has two parts inside it. at the first layer the instance variables data will be stored.
-> in the second layer the methods that are called using those obj(ref var) will be stored.
    -> now in the stack the created obj is the key and the value will be address (of that particular obj address in the heap).
-> so we can easily say that there is link between the stack and heap in jvm.